ABSTRACT
============================================================

The SMNNIP framework, stratified by the Cayley-Dickson algebra tower
R→C→H→O, produces a neural spacetime on the layer-token grid (l, τ).
This addendum addresses a specific limit case that emerged in
conversation: the exactly flat event horizon — the boundary condition
where the curvature of the neural metric tensor g_lτ goes to zero
globally, not locally.

We observe that this condition is not a degenerate case but a
structurally significant one: it corresponds precisely to the boundary
between a black hole regime (information compression, weight
convergence, representational collapse toward a VEV) and a white hole
regime (information expansion, weight decompression, representational
reconstruction from a VEV). The flat horizon is the moment of exact
symmetry between these two dynamical regimes — and it has a natural
identification in the SMNNIP framework as the Noether constraint
surface itself.

The Bekenstein-Hawking entropy of this neural horizon is computed,
and its relationship to the neural fine structure constant α_NN is
derived. The result connects directly to the Omega Constant paper
(companion document) via the fixed-point structure of the entropy
function at the horizon.

============================================================
I. THE NEURAL METRIC AND ITS CURVATURE
============================================================

In SMNNIP, the layer-token grid (l, τ) carries a natural metric
induced by the activation propagation dynamics. The neural metric
tensor is:

    g_lτ = ⟨Ψ_i(l,τ) | Ψ_i(l,τ)⟩_A

where ⟨·|·⟩_A is the inner product in the algebra A at layer l.

The Ricci scalar curvature of this metric:

    R_NN = g^lτ R_lτ

measures the degree to which the representation space curves under
the weight field dynamics. In standard gradient descent on a flat
real-valued network, R_NN = 0 identically — the representation space
is Euclidean and uncurved. This is the pathological limit.

In SMNNIP, R_NN ≠ 0 generically, because the non-Abelian structure
constants f^abc of the quaternionic and octonionic layers introduce
non-commutative curvature into the covariant derivative D_l.

The exactly flat horizon is the locus:

    R_NN = 0    with    dR_NN/dl ≠ 0

This is not the same as R_NN = 0 everywhere. It is the zero-crossing
— the layer depth l* at which the curvature changes sign.

============================================================
II. BLACK HOLE / WHITE HOLE DUALITY IN REPRESENTATION SPACE
============================================================

The sign of R_NN has a direct interpretive meaning:

    R_NN > 0 : NEURAL BLACK HOLE REGIME
    ─────────────────────────────────────
    Representation space is positively curved (like a sphere).
    Geodesics converge. Activations are pulled toward
    attractors. The network is compressing information —
    collapsing the input distribution toward a finite set
    of stable representations. Weight field has settled
    into a VEV (the Higgs mechanism is active).
    Information is being absorbed.

    R_NN < 0 : NEURAL WHITE HOLE REGIME
    ─────────────────────────────────────
    Representation space is negatively curved (like a saddle).
    Geodesics diverge. Activations spread across the
    representational manifold. The network is expanding
    information — reconstructing a rich output distribution
    from compressed internal representations. The VEV is
    being projected outward into token space.
    Information is being emitted.

    R_NN = 0 AT l* : THE FLAT HORIZON
    ─────────────────────────────────────
    The exact boundary. Neither absorbing nor emitting.
    Representational inertia is zero at this layer.
    This is the layer at which the Noether current J^a_l
    is exactly conserved with zero correction term.
    The horizon is the Noether surface made geometrically
    manifest.

The duality is not metaphorical. In general relativity, a black hole
and a white hole are the same Schwarzschild solution read in opposite
time directions. In SMNNIP, the black hole and white hole regimes
are the same network read in opposite propagation directions —
forward pass (black hole: compression) and backward pass
(white hole: reconstruction / backpropagation as emission).

The neural Yang-Mills equation:

    D_l R^{a,lτ} = g Ψ̄_i T^a Ψ_i

governs the transition. At l*, the left-hand side vanishes — the
weight field curvature has zero layer-derivative. This is the
equilibrium condition. The horizon is where the network has finished
deciding what to represent and has not yet begun emitting it.

